{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "4: Secure the RetailZero API with Access Tokens",
  "nextTour": "5: Configure CIBA in Auth0",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "What you will build",
      "description": "## What you will build\n\nZ-Assistant currently calls the RetailZero API with no authentication — any process that can reach port 3001 can read orders, trigger refunds, or access customer PII.\n\nIn this tour you will fix that using Auth0 access tokens:\n\n1. The RetailZero API will be registered as an Auth0 **Resource Server** with its own audience.\n2. When a user logs in to Z-Assistant, Auth0 will also issue an **access token** scoped to the RetailZero API and store it in the session.\n3. Every tool call will attach that token as an `Authorization: Bearer` header.\n4. The RetailZero API will validate every incoming request against Auth0's JWKS endpoint, rejecting calls with no valid token.\n\nBecause Auth0 is the authorization server for both Z-Assistant and RetailZero, no token exchange or extra network call is needed — the access token is already in the session after login.\n\nYou will make five code changes across two projects."
    },
    {
      "title": "The RetailZero API resource",
      "description": "## The RetailZero API resource\n\nThe lab extension has auto-provisioned a **RetailZero API** Resource Server in your Auth0 tenant.\n\n1. Open the [Auth0 Dashboard](https://manage.auth0.com) and go to **Applications** > **APIs**.\n2. Select **RetailZero API**.\n3. Note the **API Audience**: `https://retailzero-api/`. This is the value already set as `RETAILZERO_API_AUDIENCE` in your Z-Assistant environment.\n4. Select the **Permissions** tab and review the four scopes:\n   - `read:catalog`\n   - `read:orders`\n   - `read:customer-profile`\n   - `write:refunds`\n\nThese scopes represent what the agent is allowed to do on behalf of the user. The access token issued at login will include any scopes that the user is permitted to grant."
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/app.ts",
      "description": "## Request the RetailZero API audience at login\n\nTo get an access token for the RetailZero API, you just need to include its audience in the Auth0 authorization request. `express-openid-connect` takes care of the rest — it stores the resulting access token in the encrypted session cookie alongside the ID token.\n\nUncomment the `audience` line inside `authorizationParams`:\n\n```ts\naudience: process.env.RETAILZERO_API_AUDIENCE,\n```\n\nAfter this change, every new login will result in Auth0 issuing a JWT access token for `https://retailzero-api/` that the agent can retrieve from the session.",
      "line": 44,
      "title": "Request the RetailZero API audience at login",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 44,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 50,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "retailzero-api/src/app.ts",
      "description": "## Protect the RetailZero API\n\nThe JWT validation middleware is already written in `retailzero-api/src/middleware/auth.ts`. Open that file and review it — it uses `express-oauth2-jwt-bearer` (Auth0's official Express middleware) to validate every incoming Bearer token against your tenant's JWKS endpoint.\n\nUncomment both lines:\n\n```ts\nimport { validateToken } from './middleware/auth.js';\n```\n\nand:\n\n```ts\napp.use('/api/', validateToken);\n```\n\nOnce active, every request to any `/api/*` route must include a valid `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header signed by your Auth0 tenant. Requests without a token receive `401 Unauthorized`.",
      "line": 10,
      "title": "Protect the RetailZero API",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 10,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 18,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "retailzero-api/.env.example",
      "description": "## Configure the RetailZero API environment\n\nThe JWT middleware reads `AUTH0_DOMAIN` and `RETAILZERO_API_AUDIENCE` from the environment. Create a `.env` file for the RetailZero API:\n\n1. In a terminal:\n   ```\n   cd retailzero-api && cp .env.example .env\n   ```\n2. Open `retailzero-api/.env` and fill in your Auth0 domain:\n   ```\n   PORT=3001\n   AUTH0_DOMAIN=\"{yourTenantDomain}.auth0.com\"\n   RETAILZERO_API_AUDIENCE=\"https://retailzero-api/\"\n   ```\n3. Restart the RetailZero API:\n   ```\n   cd retailzero-api && npm run dev\n   ```\n\nThe audience value must match exactly what is configured in your Auth0 API resource.",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Configure the RetailZero API environment"
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/lib/token-exchange.ts",
      "description": "## Implement the token helper\n\nUncomment the `getRetailZeroToken()` function by replacing the TODO block with:\n\n```ts\nexport function getRetailZeroToken(): string {\n  const req = requestStore.getStore();\n  const token = (req as any)?.oidc?.accessToken?.access_token as string | undefined;\n  if (!token) throw new Error('No RetailZero access token in session — ensure RETAILZERO_API_AUDIENCE is set and the user is logged in');\n  return token;\n}\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n\n- `requestStore.getStore()` retrieves the current HTTP request from `AsyncLocalStorage` — the same mechanism that makes `getUserID()` work inside tool `execute()` functions.\n- `req.oidc.accessToken.access_token` is the JWT that Auth0 issued for the RetailZero API at login time. `express-openid-connect` stores it in the encrypted session cookie and makes it available here.\n- No network call, no token exchange — the token is already in the session.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Implement the token helper",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 3,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 15,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/tools/retailzero.ts",
      "description": "## Wire the token into the tools\n\n**Step 1 — Uncomment the import and `apiFetch` helper** (lines 3–15):\n\n```ts\nimport { getRetailZeroToken } from '../lib/token-exchange.js';\n```\n\nand:\n\n```ts\nasync function apiFetch(url: string, init?: RequestInit): Promise<Response> {\n  const token = getRetailZeroToken();\n  return fetch(url, { ...init, headers: { ...init?.headers, Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2 — Replace `fetch(` with `apiFetch(` in all five tool `execute` functions.**\n\nSearch for `// TODO (Tour 04 - Step 7): Change fetch → apiFetch` — there are five occurrences. For each one:\n\n- Change `const res = await fetch(` → `const res = await apiFetch(`\n\nThe `apiFetch` helper transparently reads the session token and adds the `Authorization` header on every call.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Wire the token into the tools",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 3,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 15,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Test the secured API",
      "description": "## Test the secured API\n\n1. Log out of Z-Assistant and log back in so the session includes the new RetailZero access token.\n2. Make sure the **RetailZero API** has been restarted with the new `.env` file (Step 5).\n3. [Start Z-Assistant](command:workbench.action.debug.start) if it isn't running.\n4. Send this prompt:\n   > _\"Search for all electronics products.\"_\n5. Z-Assistant should respond with the product list. The access token from your session was attached to the API call automatically.\n\n---\n\n**Verify the API rejects unauthenticated requests:**\n\n6. In a terminal:\n   ```\n   curl http://localhost:3001/api/products\n   ```\n7. You should receive `401 Unauthorized` — the API now enforces Auth0 token validation on every route.\n\n---\n\n**Congratulations!** Z-Assistant now acts on behalf of the logged-in user when calling the RetailZero API. Every tool call carries a session-backed access token issued by Auth0, and the API validates it on every request.\n\nNext up: Tour 5 — Configure CIBA in Auth0, which adds human-in-the-loop approval for sensitive operations on top of this foundation."
    }
  ]
